    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nOne-Off 1977 Jeep J10 Extended Cab Prototype Is Up for Auction for the First Time\n20 photos\nPhoto: Mecum Auctions\nJeep introduced its first pickup truck in 1947. Called the Willys Jeep Truck, it was joined by the Jeep Forward Control in 1956. Both were discontinued after the introduction of the more iconic Gladiator in 1962.\nOriginally launched under Willys Motors, the Gladiator was produced and sold as a Kaiser-Jeep vehicle between 1963 and 1970. The pickup truck soldiered on through AMC and Chrysler ownerships before it was discontinued after the 1988 model year.\nIn 1971, one year after Jeep was purchased by American Motors, the Gladiator name was dropped, and the truck became known as the Pickup or J-Series. The vehicles were designated as J2000 and J4000 from 1971 until 1973 and then as J10 and J20 through 1988. The 1977 pickup you see here is a J10, but it's not the average Jeep truck that rolled off the assembly line that year.\nHow so? Well, this J10 has an extended cab layout. And while it may seem like a regular production model, Jeep never offered such a version of the Gladiator . Despite its long production run of 27 years, the Gladiator was only offered as a regular cab pickup truck.\nSo what's the deal with this J10? Well, it was actually built by Jeep, but it never made it past the prototype stage. Built on a Wagoneer frame and a Cherokee Chief cab with second-row seating, the vehicle was put together to explore the potential of an extended cab version. The idea wasn't entirely new; Dodge launched an extended cab truck in 1973, while Ford joined the niche in 1974.\nThe concept wasn't entirely new for the platform either. Kaiser Argentina (IKA) built an extended cab variant of the truck for South American markets between 1963 and 1978. The local market also got a four-door double cab iteration. Neither made it to the United States.\nLike most prototypes of the era, this J10 was intended to be crushed. Luckily, the man who supervised the project, Harold \"Pete\" Johnson (the chief of AMC/Jeep R&D), decided to store it in a back room of the Toledo plant. When he retired a few years later, Johnson was allowed to purchase the truck for $1.\nThe vehicle has remained in the care of Johnson and his family ever since, getting an extensive nut-and-bolt restoration to original specs. It was refinished in the correct Golden Ginger Poly paint and retains the custom-built passenger seat that leans forward to ease access to the rear seats.\nThe Jeep spent most of its life in storage but was exhibited at the Toledo Jeep Fest, the Bantam Jeep Heritage Festival, and at the AkzoNobel booth at the SEMA Show. It's been nearly 50 years since it rolled out of Jeep's Toledo facility, and the pickup truck is up for sale for the very first time.\nThe prototype will go under the hammer at Mecum's Kissimmee 2027 auction on January 8. It's impossible to tell how much it will fetch, but there's a chance that it will become one of the most expensive vintage Gladiators ever auctioned. As of this writing, the record is held by a 1979 J10 Honcho that sold for $58,300.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nprototype Jeep Gladiator jeep j10 jeep pickup truck Extended Cab pickup truck truck pickup\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT BMW's M4 Convertible Getting a Battery-Electric Neue Klasse Makeover, New Model Spied in a Premiere",
